3. What is an Index in Pandas?
 Index = labels for rows
•	Can be numeric or custom 
•	Immutable (mostly) 
•	Enables fast lookup

4.  What is the difference between loc and iloc?
🔹 loc - 
•	Label-based 
•	Uses column/row names 
🔹 iloc -
•	Position-based 
•	Uses integer index
Example:
df.loc[0, 'name']   # label
df.iloc[0, 1]       # position

5. What is the difference between copy() and view() 

copy () - Creates a completely new object with its own memory.
Changes in copied data → ❌ do NOT affect original
Ex:
df = pd.DataFrame({'A': [1, 2, 3]})
df_copy = df.copy()

View () - Returns a reference to the original data (shares memory).
Changes in view → ✅ affect original DataFrame
Example:
df_view = df['A']   # often behaves like a view
df_view[0] = 100
print(df)


6. What is SettingWithCopyWarning?
Happens when: You modify a slice of DataFrame incorrectly
Ex: df[df['age'] > 25]['salary'] = 5000  ❌
Crct : df.loc[df['age'] > 25, 'salary'] = 5000 ✅

7. What is groupby()?
Used for aggregation  - Steps:
1.	Split 
2.	Apply 
3.	Combine
Ex: df.groupby('dept')['salary'].mean()

8. Difference between apply(), map(), applymap()?

map () : Works only on Series (single column)
Use case: Element-wise transformation
df['salary'] = df['salary'].map(lambda x: x * 2)
✔ Applies function to each value
✔ Can also use dictionary mapping

apply (): Works on Series AND DataFrame
Case 1: On Series
df['salary'].apply(lambda x: x * 2)

Case 2: On DataFrame
Operates on rows or columns
df.apply(lambda row: row['A'] + row['B'], axis=1)

applymap() : Works on: Entire DataFrame
Use case: Element-wise operation on all cells
ex: df.applymap(lambda x: x * 2)



Function	Applies on	Use
map()	Series	element-wise
apply()	Series/DataFrame	row/column
applymap()	DataFrame	element-wise

9. What is merge() vs join() vs concat()
🔹 merge()
•	SQL-like join 
•	Based on keys 
🔹 join () : DataFrame.join() joins two DataFrames based on their index (row labels) by default
•	Index-based merge 
•	Steps :  Join is done on index  , Matching indices → values combined , Non-matching → NaN
🔹 concat()
•	Stack/append data

